From cae5875f457d0ffba51a6fdbfe0ac371b6b768ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Azizbek Atajanov <33376229+azizbekatajanov@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 15:45:50 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] OpenSSL: allow connecting to servers without secure
 renegotiation

OpenSSL 3.0 made SSL_OP_LEGACY_SERVER_CONNECT opt-in, so connecting to a
server that doesn't support RFC 5746 fails with "unsafe legacy
renegotiation disabled" before anything else happens. Some
GlobalProtect gateways (older PAN-OS) are such servers. OpenSSL 1.1 and
GnuTLS builds of openconnect connect to them fine.

Set SSL_OP_LEGACY_SERVER_CONNECT unconditionally. Unsafe renegotiation
itself stays disabled, and the cipher list and security level are
unchanged; --allow-insecure-crypto is no longer needed just for this.
---
 openssl.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/openssl.c b/openssl.c
index 761cb03..978e29e 100644
--- a/openssl.c
+++ b/openssl.c
@@ -1917,6 +1917,15 @@ int openconnect_open_https(struct openconnect_info *vpninfo)
 		SSL_CTX_set_options(vpninfo->https_ctx, SSL_OP_NO_TICKET);
 #endif
 
+		/* OpenSSL 3.0 refuses to even connect to servers that don't
+		 * support RFC 5746 secure renegotiation, which some GlobalProtect
+		 * gateways (older PAN-OS) still don't. OpenSSL 1.1 and GnuTLS
+		 * both allow the initial connection. SSL_OP_LEGACY_SERVER_CONNECT
+		 * restores just that: renegotiating with such a server remains
+		 * refused (SSL_OP_ALLOW_UNSAFE_LEGACY_RENEGOTIATION is not set),
+		 * and ciphers/security level are untouched. */
+		SSL_CTX_set_options(vpninfo->https_ctx, SSL_OP_LEGACY_SERVER_CONNECT);
+
 #if OPENSSL_VERSION_NUMBER >= 0x010100000L
 		if (vpninfo->allow_insecure_crypto) {
 			/* OpenSSL versions after 1.1.0 added the notion of a "security level"
